data.content:
**Yes, C# fully supports `stackalloc` into `Span<nint>` and `Span<nuint>`.** Both work exactly as you'd expect:

```csharp
Span<nint>  nints  = stackalloc nint[4];
Span<nuint> nuints = stackalloc nuint[3];
```

On this 64-bit system, `nint`/`nuint` are 8 bytes each. The only gotcha is that `sizeof(nint)` requires an `unsafe` context because the size isn't fixed at compile time — but `stackalloc` itself works fine in safe code (when assigned directly to a `Span`).
